      "message": "This is something that I actually also considered, but I’d prefer to keep these as two commits because they introduce logically separate changes with different risk profiles.\n\n* change 517822 migrates the dependency fetching/resolution mechanism to `bzlmod` + `rules_jvm_external` while intentionally preserving the existing two-graph model and WAR contents (via exclusions and packaging filters). This is a plumbing change and serves as a safe landing point where runtime behavior is expected to remain aligned with the pre-migration setup.\n* this commit then changes the dependency model itself by unifying Gerrit and JGit onto a shared Maven graph, aligns the servlet API, and introduces root-level pin enforcement via `maven.amend_artifact` to prevent cross-module version skew. This is a semantic resolution change and therefore much harder to debug if combined with the migration step.\n\nKeeping them split improves reviewability (different concerns), bisectability, and revertability: if graph unification introduces a runtime/classpath regression during WAR assembly, we can revert that independently without losing the migration to rules_jvm_external.\n\nI agree that squashing would reduce history noise and avoid temporarily landing the transitional two-graph setup, but having an intermediate behavior-preserving step makes it significantly easier to validate the migration separately from the resolution-model change.",
